    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nCalling `.decode()` without arguments on a bytes literal crashes the compiler in `OptimizeBuiltinCalls`. `b'abc'.decode('utf8')` and `s.decode()` on a `bytes` variable compile fine.\n\n```\nt.py:2:24: Compiler crash in OptimizeBuiltinCalls\n...\n  File \".../Cython/Compiler/Optimize.py\", line 4111, in _handle_simple_method_bytes_decode\n    constant_result = string_node.constant_result.decode(encoding, error_handling)\nTypeError: decode() argument 'encoding' must be str, not None\n```\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\ndef f():\n    return b'abc'.decode()\n```\n\n`cython -3 t.py`\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nThe module compiles and `f()` returns `'abc'`, as in CPython and in Cython 3.0.12.\n\n### OS\n\nmacOS (arm64)\n\n### Python version\n\n3.11.15\n\n### Cython version\n\nmaster (c755f5c), 3.3.1, 3.2.9 and 3.1.8 crash; 3.0.12 works\n\n### Additional context\n\nThe constant decode passes `encoding=None` through when no encoding is given. Using `'utf-8'` there fixes it for me, and `tests/run/bytesmethods.pyx` passes with an added test for the literal case. I can open a PR if that is welcome.\n",
